lessons.body:
if is a fork in the road: check a condition, take one path or the other.

```rust
let age = 20;
if age >= 18 {
    println!("Welcome in!");
} else {
    println!("Sorry, adults only.");
}
```

Two Rust-specific rules:

1. The condition must be a real true/false. In Python or JavaScript, the number 3 counts as "truthy" — in Rust it does not. Write age != 0, not just age.

2. if is an EXPRESSION — it produces a value you can grab:

```rust
let n = 7;
let label = if n % 2 == 0 { "even" }
            else { "odd" };
// label is "odd"
```

Think of it as a question with an answer, not just a detour. (Both branches must answer with the same type — you can't get text from one path and a number from the other.)

For many forks at once, there's match — picture a mail sorting office where EVERY letter must end up in some box:

```rust
match n {
    0      => println!("zero"),
    1..=9  => println!("single digit"),
    _      => println!("something bigger"),
}
```

The _ box means "everything else". Forget to cover a case and the compiler refuses to continue — Rust makes "oops, I never thought of that value" impossible.

