lessons.body:
Loops repeat work so you don't have to copy-paste code. Rust gives you three, each matching a real-life situation:

1. while — "keep washing dishes WHILE dirty ones remain":

```rust
let mut dishes = 5;
while dishes > 0 {
    dishes -= 1;
}
```

2. for — "for EACH plate in the stack, do this". The friendliest and most common loop:

```rust
for i in 0..5 {
    println!("plate {i}");  // 0,1,2,3,4
}
```

Watch the range: 0..5 stops BEFORE 5 (five rounds: 0-4). Want to include the end? Add =:  1..=5 counts 1,2,3,4,5.

3. loop — a treadmill with no off switch except the break button:

```rust
let answer = loop {
    // ...keep trying something...
    break 42;  // stop AND hand back a value
};
```

That last trick is special: only loop can return a value through break (while and for cannot). Handy for "retry until it works, then give me the result".

One more button: continue skips straight to the next round — "this plate is already clean, next!".

