lessons.body:
Here is a surprise that catches every beginner: everything the user types is TEXT, even when it looks like a number. Type 42 and your program receives the string "42" — three characters, not a value you can do math on.

```rust
let text = "42";
let n = text + 1;   // ❌ you cannot add 1 to text
```

The bridge from text to number is .parse():

```rust
let n: i32 = "42".parse().expect("not a number");
let sum = n + 1;    // ✅ now it is a real 43
```

Two things make parse work. First, it needs to know WHICH number type you want — Rust cannot guess. You tell it either with a type on the variable (let n: i32) or with the "turbofish":

```rust
let n = "42".parse::<i32>().expect("not a number");
//                  ^^^^^ turbofish: "parse it as i32"
```

Second, parsing can FAIL — what is "hello" as a number? So parse returns a Result, just like read_line. For now, .expect handles the bad case by crashing with a message; the error-handling course will show how to recover gracefully instead.

The full pattern, reading a typed number, ties this lesson to the last one:

```rust
let mut line = String::new();
io::stdin().read_line(&mut line).expect("read failed");
let age: u32 = line.trim().parse().expect("please type a number");
```

Notice the .trim() BEFORE .parse() — without it the leftover newline makes parsing fail. trim, then parse: the reliable order for turning keyboard input into numbers.

