lessons.body:
Some values never change for the whole life of a program — the number of hours in a day, a maximum score, the value of pi. For these, Rust offers const: a name carved in stone.

```rust
const MAX_PLAYERS: u32 = 4;
const PI: f64 = 3.14159;
```

Three rules separate a const from a normal let:

1. The type is ALWAYS required. Rust will not infer it — you must write : u32.
2. The value must be known at COMPILE TIME. No reading a file or asking the user; it has to be a fixed expression.
3. The name is SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE by convention — all caps with underscores, so constants stand out in code.

Constants can live anywhere, including OUTSIDE any function, at the top level — perfect for settings shared across your whole program:

```rust
const TAX_RATE: f64 = 0.08;

fn main() {
    let total = 50.0;
    println!("{}", total * TAX_RATE);
}
```

There is a close cousin: static. It also names a fixed global value, but with one difference — a static has a single fixed location in memory for the entire run, while a const is effectively pasted in wherever it is used. For everyday "this never changes" values, reach for const; static is for the rarer cases where you genuinely need one shared memory address.

The simple takeaway: use const for fixed, compile-time values; spell out the type; SHOUT the name.

