lessons.body:
Every variable has a lifetime tied to the { } block it was born in. This is its SCOPE: the region of code where the name is alive and usable. Step outside the closing brace and the name is gone.

```rust
fn main() {
    let x = 5;          // x is born
    {
        let y = 10;     // y is born, inside an inner block
        println!("{x} {y}");  // both alive here
    }                   // y dies at this brace
    println!("{x}");    // fine — x still alive
    // println!("{y}"); // ❌ y is out of scope, name unknown
}
```

A block is just a pair of braces grouping statements. Inner blocks can SEE the variables of outer blocks, but not the reverse — the inside knows about the outside, never the other way round.

This connects to shadowing from the variables lesson. Inside a new scope you can declare a fresh variable with the same name, and it temporarily HIDES the outer one — without touching it:

```rust
let n = 5;
{
    let n = n * 10;   // a new n, only inside this block
    println!("{n}");  // 50
}
println!("{n}");      // 5 — the outer n was never changed
```

The inner n is a separate variable that happens to share a label. When its block ends, it vanishes and the original n re-emerges, untouched.

Why does any of this matter? Scope is the heartbeat of Rust. Values are cleaned up the instant their scope ends — no garbage collector needed. Understanding where a variable lives and dies is the foundation the entire ownership system is built on.

