lessons.body:
Arrays are fixed in size. But most of the time you do not know up front how many things you will have — items in a cart, scores in a game, lines from a file. For that you need a Vec ("vector"): a list that GROWS and shrinks at runtime.

```rust
let mut cart = Vec::new();   // empty, will hold... (inferred)
cart.push("apple");
cart.push("bread");
cart.push("milk");
println!("{} items", cart.len());   // 3
```

Or build one immediately with the vec! macro:

```rust
let mut scores = vec![10, 20, 30];   // Vec<i32>
```

Note the `mut` — adding or removing items CHANGES the Vec, so it must be mutable. The everyday operations:

```rust
scores.push(40);           // add to the end
let last = scores.pop();   // remove & return the last -> Some(40)
let first = scores[0];     // index (panics if out of range)
let safe = scores.get(0);  // Option — None if out of range, no panic
```

Indexing with [] panics on a bad index; .get() returns an Option instead — prefer .get() when the index might be invalid.

A Vec works seamlessly with everything you have learned:

```rust
for s in &scores {              // borrow to iterate (keep the Vec)
    println!("{s}");
}
let total: i32 = scores.iter().sum();
let doubled: Vec<i32> = scores.iter().map(|n| n * 2).collect();
```

And remember slices: a function taking &[i32] accepts a &Vec too, so the array helpers from the last lesson work on vectors unchanged.

The mental model: array when the size is FIXED and known; Vec when the list GROWS. Vec is the workhorse collection of Rust — you will use it constantly. (It has many more powers — sorting, filtering, removing — explored fully in the Collections course; this is your foundation.)

