lessons.body:
Functions are where ownership rules become a daily habit. The signature alone tells you what happens to your data — read it like a contract.

THREE ways to pass a value:

1. By value — give it away. The function takes ownership; your variable is gone afterwards.

```rust
fn consume(s: String) { println!("{s}"); }   // s dies here

let name = String::from("Sam");
consume(name);
// name is MOVED — using it now is an error
```

2. By shared reference — lend it to read. Add &. The function peeks; you keep ownership.

```rust
fn show(s: &String) { println!("{s}"); }

let name = String::from("Sam");
show(&name);
println!("{name}");   // ✅ still yours
```

3. By mutable reference — lend it to change. Add &mut.

```rust
fn shout(s: &mut String) { s.push_str("!!!"); }

let mut name = String::from("hey");
shout(&mut name);
println!("{name}");   // "hey!!!"
```

The rule of thumb professionals use: borrow by default (&), borrow mutably when you must change (&mut), take ownership only when the function genuinely needs to KEEP or consume the value (storing it, transforming it into something returned).

Copy types are the gentle exception — i32, bool, char and friends are copied, so passing them by value leaves your original perfectly usable. Only heap-owning types like String and Vec actually move.

