lessons.body:
The mutable reference (&mut) is powerful and tightly controlled. Understanding its one big rule unlocks most "cannot borrow" errors.

THE RULE: while a &mut reference is alive, NOTHING else may touch the value — no other &mut, and no plain & either.

```rust
let mut s = String::from("hi");
let a = &mut s;
let b = &mut s;   // ❌ second mutable borrow
println!("{a} {b}");
```

Why so strict? A mutable reference promises EXCLUSIVE access. If two existed, both could change the data at once — or one could read half-written data. Rust guarantees that whoever holds a &mut is the only one looking.

The relief: borrows end at their LAST USE, not the closing brace (this is "non-lexical lifetimes"). So this is fine:

```rust
let mut s = String::from("hi");
let a = &mut s;
a.push_str("!");      // last use of a — borrow ends HERE
let b = &mut s;       // ✅ now allowed
b.push_str("?");
```

Because a is never used after push_str, its exclusive borrow is already over by the time b is created. Rust is checking real usage, not just scopes.

A handy move: you can RE-BORROW through a mutable reference to pass it along:

```rust
fn tweak(s: &mut String) { s.push('.'); }

let r = &mut s;
tweak(r);     // reborrow: r still valid afterwards
```

Master "one exclusive borrow, ending at last use" and the borrow checker stops feeling random.

