lessons.body:
You have heard that values are "cleaned up" when their owner goes out of scope. The mechanism behind that is the Drop trait — Rust's automatic janitor.

When a value owning resources (heap memory, a file, a network socket) reaches the end of its scope, Rust automatically runs cleanup code: freeing the memory, closing the file. You almost never call this yourself — it just happens, at a precise, predictable moment.

```rust
{
    let s = String::from("data");   // owns heap memory
}   // <- s dropped HERE: its heap memory is freed automatically
```

You can SEE it by implementing Drop for your own type:

```rust
struct Guard { name: String }

impl Drop for Guard {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        println!("Dropping {}", self.name);
    }
}

fn main() {
    let _a = Guard { name: String::from("A") };
    let _b = Guard { name: String::from("B") };
}   // prints "Dropping B" then "Dropping A"
```

Two details worth knowing:

• Order is REVERSE of creation — last created, first dropped (like un-stacking plates). B was made last, so B drops first.
• You cannot call .drop() yourself (Rust forbids it, to avoid double-cleanup). To drop something early, use the free function std::mem::drop(value), which takes ownership and lets it fall out of scope immediately.

This is RAII — "resource acquisition is initialization" — and it is why Rust needs no garbage collector and no manual free(): cleanup is tied to ownership, guaranteed by the compiler.

