lessons.body:
Ownership does not only flow INTO functions — it flows back OUT through return values. Returning a value MOVES its ownership to the caller.

```rust
fn make_greeting() -> String {
    let s = String::from("hello");
    s   // ownership moves OUT to whoever called us
}

let g = make_greeting();   // g now owns the String
```

This is how the dangling-reference problem is avoided cleanly: instead of returning a reference to a local (which dies), you return the owned value itself, and ownership rides along with it.

Sometimes a function borrows a value, uses it, and needs to give it BACK. The old way was to return it:

```rust
fn measure(s: String) -> (String, usize) {
    let len = s.len();
    (s, len)   // hand the String back alongside the answer
}

let (s, len) = measure(String::from("hi"));
```

Returning a TUPLE lets a function produce several values at once — and the let destructures them into separate variables. Handy, though for "just let me read it" cases a plain & reference is usually cleaner than shuttling ownership back and forth.

The mental model: think of ownership as a baton in a relay. It can be handed in (parameters), handed back (return values), or simply lent for a moment (&). Return values are the "handing back" — and they always move, never copy (for non-Copy types).

