lessons.body:
So far our data has been loose change — an age here, a name there. A struct is a labeled form that keeps related facts together, like one contact card instead of three sticky notes:

```rust
struct User {
    name: String,
    age: u32,
}
```

This defines the BLANK form: every User has a name box and an age box. Now fill one in and read it back:

```rust
let u = User {
    name: String::from("Ferris"),
    age: 5,
};

println!("{} is {}", u.name, u.age);
```

Dot syntax (u.name) opens a labeled box. That's it — structs are 90% "group my stuff and give the parts names".

Two conveniences you'll meet:

```rust
// tuple struct: boxes numbered, not named
struct Point(i32, i32);
let p = Point(3, 4);   // p.0 is 3, p.1 is 4

// update syntax: "like u, but age is 6"
let older = User { age: 6, ..u };
```

The ..u reads as "fill every box I didn't mention from u" — perfect for making a tweaked copy of a form without retyping it.

