lessons.body:
A struct holds the data; an impl block teaches it ABILITIES. Think of it as the difference between a paper contact card and a smart one that knows how to call itself:

```rust
struct Rect { w: u32, h: u32 }

impl Rect {
    fn area(&self) -> u32 {
        self.w * self.h
    }
}

let r = Rect { w: 3, h: 4 };
println!("{}", r.area()); // 12
```

That `&self` is the method's view of "the struct I was called on" — and the & rules you already know apply:

• &self — "let me LOOK at the data" (read-only, most common)
• &mut self — "hand me the pen" (the method changes fields)
• self — "I'm taking ownership" (rare; consumes the value)

```rust
impl Rect {
    fn grow(&mut self, by: u32) {
        self.w += by;   // needs &mut self
    }
}
```

One more pattern you've already used without knowing: functions in impl WITHOUT self are associated functions — called on the type with ::, and by convention `new` builds instances:

```rust
impl Rect {
    fn new(w: u32, h: u32) -> Rect {
        Rect { w, h }
    }
}

let r = Rect::new(3, 4); // like String::from!
```

