lessons.body:
A struct says "all of these fields at once". An enum says "exactly ONE of these shapes" — like a parcel that is either ordered, shipped, or delivered, never two at once:

```rust
enum Status {
    Ordered,
    Shipped(String),        // carries a tracking code!
    Delivered { days: u32 } // variants can hold data
}

let s = Status::Shipped(String::from("TRK123"));
```

Notice: each variant can CARRY different data. That is the superpower — the shape of the data follows the state.

Now the most important enum in all of Rust. Other languages have null — "there is nothing here" — and forgetting to check for it crashes a million apps a day (its inventor calls it the billion-dollar mistake). Rust has no null. Instead:

```rust
enum Option<T> {
    Some(T),  // there IS a value
    None,     // there is not
}

let found: Option<u32> = lookup("ferris");
match found {
    Some(score) => println!("score: {score}"),
    None => println!("no such player"),
}
```

The genius part: a maybe-missing value has a DIFFERENT TYPE than a definitely-there value. You physically cannot use an Option<u32> as a u32 without first handling the None case — the compiler makes forgetting impossible. Where other languages crash at runtime, Rust refuses at compile time.

