lessons.body:
You've used match as a sorting office. Now the power tools — patterns don't just CHECK shapes, they take them apart:

```rust
let point = (0, 7);
match point {
    (0, 0) => println!("origin"),
    (0, y) => println!("on y-axis at {y}"),
    (x, 0) => println!("on x-axis at {x}"),
    (x, y) => println!("at {x}, {y}"),
}
```

Each pattern destructures the tuple AND binds names to the pieces. Works on structs and enums too — unpack and use in one step.

Arms can also carry a guard — an extra if:

```rust
match age {
    n if n < 13 => "kid",
    n if n < 20 => "teenager",
    _ => "adult",
};
```

And when you only care about ONE case, a full match feels heavy. Meet match's little siblings:

```rust
// if let: "IF it fits this pattern, run this"
if let Some(score) = lookup("ferris") {
    println!("found: {score}");
}

// while let: "keep going WHILE it fits"
while let Some(item) = stack.pop() {
    println!("popped {item}");
}
```

Rule of thumb: many cases → match (exhaustive, compiler-checked). One case you care about → if let. Draining something until empty → while let.

