lessons.body:
A struct holds DATA. To give it BEHAVIOR — things it can do — you attach methods in an impl block. This is how Rust groups "the thing" with "what the thing can do" without needing classes.

```rust
struct Rectangle { width: f64, height: f64 }

impl Rectangle {
    // a METHOD — takes &self, the instance it is called on
    fn area(&self) -> f64 {
        self.width * self.height
    }
}

let r = Rectangle { width: 3.0, height: 4.0 };
println!("{}", r.area());   // 12.0  — called with dot syntax
```

The magic word is `self`, and it comes in three flavours mirroring ownership:

• `&self` — borrow to READ. The overwhelmingly common case (like area above).
• `&mut self` — borrow to MODIFY the instance.
• `self` — TAKE ownership (consumes the value; rarer, for transformations).

```rust
impl Rectangle {
    fn scale(&mut self, factor: f64) {   // changes the instance
        self.width *= factor;
        self.height *= factor;
    }
}
```

There is also a method WITHOUT self — an ASSOCIATED FUNCTION. The classic is a constructor named `new`, called with `::` instead of a dot:

```rust
impl Rectangle {
    fn new(w: f64, h: f64) -> Self {     // Self = Rectangle
        Rectangle { width: w, height: h }
    }
}

let r = Rectangle::new(3.0, 4.0);   // :: not .
```

The pattern to internalize: data in the struct, behavior in impl, `&self` to read, `&mut self` to change, and `Type::new(...)` as the conventional way to build one.

