lessons.body:
Not every struct needs named fields. Rust offers two leaner shapes for when names would just be noise.

TUPLE STRUCTS have fields identified by POSITION, not name — like a tuple with a type name stamped on it:

```rust
struct Point(i32, i32);
struct Rgb(u8, u8, u8);

let origin = Point(0, 0);
let red = Rgb(255, 0, 0);

println!("{}", origin.0);   // access by index, like a tuple
```

Their best use is the NEWTYPE pattern: wrapping a single value to give it a distinct type. This is a quiet superpower for correctness.

```rust
struct Meters(f64);
struct Seconds(f64);

fn speed(d: Meters, t: Seconds) -> f64 { d.0 / t.0 }
```

Now you physically cannot pass seconds where meters are expected — the compiler rejects it, even though both wrap an f64. A whole class of unit-confusion bugs (the kind that crashed a Mars probe) becomes impossible.

UNIT STRUCTS have NO fields at all:

```rust
struct Marker;
```

Why would an empty struct be useful? When you need a TYPE but no data — typically to implement a trait on "nothing", or as a state marker, or a zero-size token. It costs zero bytes.

The guide: named-field structs for data with meaningful field names; tuple structs for small positional bundles and newtype wrappers; unit structs when you need a type but no data. Reaching for a newtype instead of a raw f64 or String is a hallmark of robust Rust — it lets the type system catch mix-ups for free.

