lessons.body:
Many operations can fail: parsing text, opening a file, dividing. Languages with exceptions hide that failure until it explodes at runtime. Rust puts it right in the TYPE with the Result enum — failure you cannot forget to handle.

Result has exactly two variants:

```rust
enum Result<T, E> {
    Ok(T),    // success, carrying a value of type T
    Err(E),   // failure, carrying an error of type E
}
```

A function that might fail RETURNS a Result, so the caller is forced to deal with both outcomes:

```rust
fn divide(a: f64, b: f64) -> Result<f64, String> {
    if b == 0.0 {
        Err(String::from("divide by zero"))
    } else {
        Ok(a / b)
    }
}
```

To unpack it, match on the two cases — and the compiler will not let you forget the Err arm:

```rust
match divide(10.0, 2.0) {
    Ok(value) => println!("got {value}"),
    Err(msg)  => println!("error: {msg}"),
}
```

For quick code you can `.unwrap()` (gives the Ok value, but PANICS on Err) or `.expect("message")` (same, with your own panic message). Use these in prototypes and tests; in real code, prefer to handle the Err.

The killer feature is the `?` OPERATOR. Inside a function that returns Result, `?` unwraps an Ok or, on Err, RETURNS that error immediately to the caller:

```rust
fn parse_two(a: &str, b: &str) -> Result<i32, std::num::ParseIntError> {
    let x: i32 = a.parse()?;   // on error, return it now
    let y: i32 = b.parse()?;
    Ok(x + y)
}
```

That one `?` replaces a pile of match statements, letting errors flow up cleanly. Result + ? is how Rust does error handling: explicit, type-checked, impossible to ignore — yet ergonomic.

