lessons.body:
An array is an egg carton — fixed slots, decided forever at compile time. A Vec is a backpack: it grows as you stuff things in.

```rust
let mut v: Vec<i32> = Vec::new();
v.push(10);
v.push(20);
v.push(30);
println!("{}", v.len()); // 3

let v2 = vec![1, 2, 3]; // shortcut macro
```

Reading elements has two flavors, and choosing between them is a tiny design decision:

```rust
let third = v[2];      // direct: PANICS if out of bounds
let third = v.get(2);  // careful: returns Option
```

Use `[i]` when the index is definitely valid (you control it), `.get(i)` when it came from outside (user input, calculations) — then None is just a case to handle, not a crash.

Looping borrows the backpack rather than emptying it:

```rust
let mut sum = 0;
for x in &v {        // note the & !
    sum += x;
}
// v still usable here
```

Forget the & and the for loop MOVES the Vec (ownership lesson déjà vu) — gone after the loop. With &mut you can even modify each item in place. Vec is the workhorse of Rust collections; most "list of things" code is a Vec plus these four moves: push, index/get, loop, len.

