lessons.body:
Rust splits all failures into two families, and choosing the right one is half of error handling:

1. Expected failures — file missing, bad user input. These get Result (next lesson).
2. "This should be IMPOSSIBLE" — a bug. These get panic!: pull the fire alarm, stop the program, print where it died.

```rust
fn divide(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {
    if b == 0 {
        panic!("divide by zero: {a}/{b}");
    }
    a / b
}
```

A panic is loud on purpose: better a clear crash pointing at the bug than silently wrong math reaching production.

You've met panic's little helpers already:

```rust
let n: i32 = "42".parse().unwrap();
//                        ^ panic if parse failed

let n: i32 = "42".parse()
    .expect("hardcoded number must parse");
//   ^ same, but with YOUR message
```

unwrap says "crash if this is Err/None". expect says the same WITH a note explaining why you were sure — future-you reads that note at the crash site, so prefer expect.

When is crashing okay? In quick experiments and tests, freely. For genuinely impossible states ("this list was literally just filled"), reasonably. In library code and servers — almost never: that is Result territory, next lesson.

(Bonus debugging tip: run with RUST_BACKTRACE=1 to see the full trail of calls that led to a panic.)

