lessons.body:
Option says "maybe there is no value". Result says more: "this worked — OR here is exactly WHY it failed":

```rust
enum Result<T, E> {
    Ok(T),    // success, carrying the value
    Err(E),   // failure, carrying the reason
}
```

Functions that touch the messy outside world — files, networks, parsing — return Result, and you handle both arms:

```rust
let n: Result<i32, _> = "42".parse();
match n {
    Ok(v) => println!("number: {v}"),
    Err(e) => println!("not a number: {e}"),
}
```

Now imagine a function with FIVE fallible steps — five nested matches? No. The ? operator is the magic wand:

```rust
fn read_age(text: &str) -> Result<u8, String> {
    let n: u8 = text.trim().parse()
        .map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;
    //                              ^ the hero
    Ok(n)
}
```

? means: "if Ok, unwrap and keep going; if Err, RETURN it to my caller right now." Errors bubble UP through the call chain until someone is positioned to handle them — main shows the user a message, a server returns a 400, a game retries.

One rule: ? only works inside functions that return Result (or Option) — it must have somewhere to forward the error. The compiler reminds you instantly if you forget.

This is THE Rust error story: failures are values, ? moves them, the type system guarantees nobody forgets. No exceptions, no surprises, no silent nulls.

