lessons.body:
HashMap is fast but UNORDERED — iterate it and the keys come out in a scrambled, unpredictable order. When you need the keys SORTED, reach for its sibling: BTreeMap.

```rust
use std::collections::BTreeMap;

let mut scores = BTreeMap::new();
scores.insert("charlie", 85);
scores.insert("alice", 92);
scores.insert("bob", 78);

for (name, score) in &scores {
    println!("{name}: {score}");
}
// ALWAYS prints alice, bob, charlie — sorted by key
```

The API is nearly identical to HashMap — insert, get, contains_key, the entry API — so switching is easy. The difference is the guarantee: a BTreeMap keeps its keys in sorted order at all times.

The trade-off:
• HashMap — O(1) average lookups, NO order. The default choice.
• BTreeMap — O(log n) lookups (a hair slower), keys always sorted.

When is the order worth it? Whenever you need:
• To iterate in sorted order (leaderboards, sorted reports).
• RANGE queries — "all entries between B and M":

```rust
for (k, v) in scores.range("b".."m") {
    // only keys in that range, in order
}
```

That range power is unique to BTreeMap; a HashMap simply cannot do it. There is a matching BTreeSet for sorted unique values (the ordered cousin of HashSet).

The decision rule: default to HashMap for raw speed; switch to BTreeMap the moment you care about sorted iteration or range queries. Same mental model, one guarantees order.

