lessons.body:
A constant need with maps: "update the value for this key, or insert a starting value if the key is new". The clumsy way needs a contains_key check, a get, and branching. Rust has an elegant one-liner: the ENTRY API.

The frequent case — COUNTING things:

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;

let mut counts: HashMap<&str, i32> = HashMap::new();
for word in text.split_whitespace() {
    *counts.entry(word).or_insert(0) += 1;
}
```

Read entry(word).or_insert(0) as: "give me a mutable reference to this key's value, creating it as 0 if absent". The leading `*` dereferences that reference so `+= 1` updates the value in place. One line replaces a whole if/else.

The entry API has several finishers:

• .or_insert(value) — use this default if the key is missing.
• .or_insert_with(|| expensive()) — like or_insert, but only RUNS the closure when actually needed (lazy, avoids wasted work).
• .or_default() — insert the type's Default (0, "", empty Vec…).
• .and_modify(|v| ...).or_insert(start) — modify if present, otherwise insert a starting value.

A powerful combo is building a map of LISTS (grouping):

```rust
let mut groups: HashMap<char, Vec<&str>> = HashMap::new();
for word in words {
    let first = word.chars().next().unwrap();
    groups.entry(first).or_default().push(word);
}
```

Each new first-letter key gets a fresh empty Vec, then the word is pushed. Grouping in three lines. Whenever you catch yourself about to write "check if key exists, then insert or update", stop — the entry API already does it, more clearly and with one lookup instead of two.

