lessons.body:
You write a function that finds the largest i32. Tomorrow you need the largest f64. Copy-paste the function, change the type… stop. That itch is what generics scratch: write the logic ONCE, with the type as a blank to fill in — a cookie cutter that works on any dough:

```rust
fn largest<T: PartialOrd>(list: &[T]) -> &T {
    let mut max = &list[0];
    for x in list {
        if x > max { max = x; }
    }
    max
}

largest(&[3, 7, 2]);          // T becomes i32
largest(&[1.5, 0.2, 9.9]);    // T becomes f64
```

Reading the signature: `<T>` declares "T is a type to be determined". The `T: PartialOrd` part is your trait-license check from the traits lesson — "any type, as long as it can be compared with >". Without the bound, the compiler rejects `x > max`: it refuses to assume abilities T might not have.

Structs take blanks too:

```rust
struct Point<T> {
    x: T,
    y: T,
}

let ints = Point { x: 1, y: 2 };
let floats = Point { x: 0.5, y: 1.5 };
```

Plot twist: you've used generics since lesson one. Vec<T>, Option<T>, Result<T, E>, HashMap<K, V> — all cookie cutters.

And the price of this flexibility? Zero. At compile time Rust stamps out a real, concrete copy for each type you actually use (called monomorphization) — generic code runs exactly as fast as hand-written copies. Abstraction without the bill.

