lessons.body:
Think about a driver's license. It doesn't matter WHO you are — student, chef, astronaut — if you hold the license, you're allowed to drive, because the license certifies a specific skill.

A trait is that license for types. It declares a skill, and any type can earn it:

```rust
trait Speak {
    fn speak(&self) -> String;
}
```

"To hold the Speak license, you must provide a speak() method." Now let two completely different types earn it:

```rust
struct Crab;
struct Robot;

impl Speak for Crab {
    fn speak(&self) -> String {
        "clack clack!".into()
    }
}

impl Speak for Robot {
    fn speak(&self) -> String {
        "BEEP.".into()
    }
}
```

Read impl Speak for Crab out loud: "implement Speak FOR Crab" — the syntax is the sentence.

The payoff: functions can now ask for the LICENSE instead of a specific type:

```rust
fn introduce(x: impl Speak) {
    println!("It says: {}", x.speak());
}

introduce(Crab);   // works
introduce(Robot);  // also works!
```

introduce doesn't care who shows up — crab, robot, whatever — as long as they're licensed to Speak. (You'll also see fn show<T: Speak>(x: T) — same meaning, different spelling.)

Bonus: traits can include a ready-made default method, which types get for free or can override. If you've used interfaces in Java or TypeScript, traits will feel familiar — then keep surprising you with extra power.

