lessons.body:
The scariest-looking Rust syntax, and the most misunderstood. Deep breath: lifetimes don't DO anything. They are labels that DESCRIBE how long references are valid, so the compiler can check your borrows make sense.

Think of library due-dates: a borrowed book's due-date can never outlast the library owning it. Every reference in Rust has such a due-date — its lifetime. Usually the compiler figures them out silently (this is why you've written hundreds of references without seeing one).

But sometimes it genuinely can't tell. Look:

```rust
fn longest(a: &str, b: &str) -> &str {
    if a.len() > b.len() { a } else { b }
}
// ❌ whose due-date does the OUTPUT have?
```

The returned reference is sometimes a, sometimes b — so how long is it valid? The compiler refuses to guess. You answer with a label:

```rust
fn longest<'a>(a: &'a str, b: &'a str) -> &'a str {
    if a.len() > b.len() { a } else { b }
}
```

Read 'a as "lifetime a": "both inputs and the output share a due-date — the output is valid only while BOTH inputs still are." That's it. No runtime cost, no behavior change — purely a description the compiler verifies.

This is also why returning a reference to a local variable can never work: the local dies at the closing brace, so any reference to it would have an expired due-date. (Return the owned value instead.)

One special label: `'static` means "valid for the whole program" — string literals have it, since they're baked into the binary.

