lessons.body:
A generic <T> alone means "any type at all" — but usually you need T to be able to DO something: be printed, compared, added. Trait bounds say "any type that implements this trait", unlocking those abilities inside the function.

```rust
use std::fmt::Display;

fn announce<T: Display>(item: T) {
    println!("Here it is: {item}");   // Display lets us use {}
}
```

`T: Display` reads "T must implement Display". Without that bound, `println!("{item}")` would not compile — the compiler cannot assume an arbitrary T can be printed.

Need MULTIPLE abilities? Combine bounds with +:

```rust
fn show_and_compare<T: Display + PartialOrd>(a: T, b: T) {
    if a < b { println!("{a} is smaller"); }  // PartialOrd gives <
}
```

When bounds pile up and the signature gets noisy, move them to a `where` clause — same meaning, more readable:

```rust
fn process<T, U>(a: T, b: U) -> String
where
    T: Display + Clone,
    U: Display,
{
    format!("{a} and {b}")
}
```

There is also the `impl Trait` shorthand for simple cases — "some type that implements this":

```rust
fn make_greeting() -> impl Display { "hello" }
fn print_it(x: impl Display) { println!("{x}"); }
```

The mental model: a generic is a PROMISE about what a type can do, and trait bounds are how you spell out that promise. The payoff is "monomorphization" — the compiler stamps out a specialized version for each concrete type you actually use, so generic code runs exactly as fast as hand-written non-generic code. Zero-cost abstraction again.

