lessons.body:
A trait is a contract: "any type implementing me provides these methods". But a trait can also provide DEFAULT implementations, so implementers get behavior for free and only override what they need.

```rust
trait Greet {
    fn name(&self) -> String;            // required — must implement

    fn hello(&self) -> String {          // default — free!
        format!("Hello, I am {}", self.name())
    }
}

struct Dog;
impl Greet for Dog {
    fn name(&self) -> String { "Rex".to_string() }
    // hello() comes for free from the default
}

let d = Dog;
println!("{}", d.hello());   // "Hello, I am Rex"
```

Dog only implemented `name`; it inherited `hello` automatically. This is how the standard library gives you so much from a single method — implement the core, get the conveniences.

A default method can CALL the required methods (like hello calls name), composing rich behavior on top of a small required surface. The classic real example is Iterator: you implement only `next()`, and dozens of methods (map, filter, sum, collect…) all come from defaults built on next().

Implementers may still OVERRIDE a default when they want different behavior:

```rust
impl Greet for Robot {
    fn name(&self) -> String { "Unit 7".to_string() }
    fn hello(&self) -> String { "BEEP BOOP".to_string() }  // override
}
```

The design principle this teaches: keep the REQUIRED methods minimal (the irreducible core), and offer rich DEFAULTS on top. Implementers do the least work, yet every type gets the full API. It is the secret behind Rust's ergonomic, composable traits.

