lessons.body:
Generics with trait bounds are resolved at COMPILE time — each concrete type gets its own stamped-out copy. But sometimes you want a collection of DIFFERENT types that share a trait, decided at RUNTIME. For that you need trait objects: `dyn Trait`.

The problem they solve: a Vec must hold ONE type. You cannot put a Dog and a Cat in a `Vec<T>`. But you CAN if they share a trait:

```rust
trait Animal { fn speak(&self) -> String; }

struct Dog; impl Animal for Dog { fn speak(&self) -> String { "Woof".into() } }
struct Cat; impl Animal for Cat { fn speak(&self) -> String { "Meow".into() } }

let animals: Vec<Box<dyn Animal>> = vec![
    Box::new(Dog),
    Box::new(Cat),
];
for a in &animals {
    println!("{}", a.speak());   // calls the right one at runtime
}
```

`Box<dyn Animal>` means "a heap-allocated something that implements Animal". The Box is needed because different types have different sizes; the Box gives a uniform pointer-sized handle. `dyn` marks it as a trait object resolved dynamically.

The trade-off — STATIC vs DYNAMIC dispatch:
• Generics (`impl Trait` / `<T: Trait>`) = STATIC dispatch. Resolved at compile time, fully inlinable, zero overhead, but every type is monomorphized separately.
• Trait objects (`dyn Trait`) = DYNAMIC dispatch. One function handles all types via a runtime vtable lookup — a tiny cost, but enables heterogeneous collections and smaller binaries.

The rule of thumb: prefer generics for performance and single-type cases; reach for `dyn` when you genuinely need a mixed bag of types behind one trait (plugin lists, UI widget trees, "a Vec of different shapes"). Not every type works as a trait object — the trait must be "object safe" (roughly: no generic methods, methods take self by reference) — but most everyday traits qualify.

