}

lessons.body:
A handful of standard-library traits show up everywhere. Implementing the right one makes YOUR types feel like built-in ones — they print, convert and integrate seamlessly.

DISPLAY — for user-facing, pretty output with {} (Debug, by contrast, is the developer-facing {:?}):

```rust
use std::fmt;

struct Temperature { celsius: f64 }

impl fmt::Display for Temperature {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter) -> fmt::Result {
        write!(f, "{}°C", self.celsius)
    }
}
// now: println!("{}", temp) prints "23°C"
```

FROM / INTO — for type conversions. Implement `From` and you get `Into` for free (the standard library wires it up):

```rust
struct Celsius(f64);
struct Fahrenheit(f64);

impl From<Celsius> for Fahrenheit {
    fn from(c: Celsius) -> Self {
        Fahrenheit(c.0 * 9.0 / 5.0 + 32.0)
    }
}

let f = Fahrenheit::from(Celsius(100.0));
let f2: Fahrenheit = Celsius(0.0).into();   // Into comes free
```

`From` is also the backbone of ergonomic error handling — the `?` operator automatically converts errors using From, which is why `?` "just works" across error types.

Other everyday traits to recognize:
• Default — `Type::default()` for a sensible zero/empty value.
• PartialEq / PartialOrd — equality and ordering (usually derived).
• Iterator — implement next(), get the whole adapter toolbox.
• FromStr — enables `"42".parse::<T>()` for your own types.

The takeaway: these traits are the SHARED VOCABULARY of Rust. When your type speaks them, it plugs into the entire ecosystem — formatting macros, conversions, the ? operator, collections — for free. Implementing Display and From on your core types is a small effort with an outsized payoff in how natural the type feels to use.

