lessons.body:
Generics are not just for functions — STRUCTS can be generic too, letting one definition work for any type. This is exactly how Vec<T>, Option<T> and HashMap<K, V> are built.

```rust
struct Pair<T> {
    first: T,
    second: T,
}

let ints = Pair { first: 1, second: 2 };         // Pair<i32>
let words = Pair { first: "a", second: "b" };    // Pair<&str>
```

One Pair definition, reused for every type. The compiler infers T from the values.

You can have MULTIPLE type parameters when fields differ:

```rust
struct Entry<K, V> {
    key: K,
    value: V,
}
```

Methods on a generic struct repeat the parameter on the impl:

```rust
impl<T> Pair<T> {
    fn first(&self) -> &T { &self.first }
}
```

Read `impl<T> Pair<T>` as "for any T, here are methods on Pair<T>". The <T> after impl introduces the generic; Pair<T> says which type the methods belong to.

The powerful part: you can add bounded methods that exist ONLY for types meeting a constraint:

```rust
impl<T: PartialOrd + Copy> Pair<T> {
    fn larger(&self) -> T {
        if self.first > self.second { self.first } else { self.second }
    }
}
```

`larger` exists only when T is comparable. A Pair of non-comparable types simply will not have that method — the capability appears exactly where it makes sense, enforced at compile time.

The big idea: generic structs let you write a container or wrapper ONCE and use it with any type, with full type safety and zero runtime cost (each concrete type is monomorphized). Combined with trait bounds on impls, you get APIs that offer exactly the right methods for exactly the right types — the foundation of Rust's elegant standard collections.

