lessons.body:
You have consumed iterators with map, filter and collect. Now flip it around: make YOUR type iterable. The magic is that you implement just ONE method — next() — and the entire adapter toolbox (map, filter, sum, take, collect…) comes along for free, via default methods on the Iterator trait.

The Iterator trait, in essence:

```rust
trait Iterator {
    type Item;                       // what it yields
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item>;  // the ONE method
}
```

next() returns Some(value) for each step, then None when finished. Here is a counter that yields 1, 2, 3:

```rust
struct Counter { count: u32 }

impl Iterator for Counter {
    type Item = u32;
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<u32> {
        if self.count < 3 {
            self.count += 1;
            Some(self.count)
        } else {
            None     // signals "done"
        }
    }
}
```

That is it. Now Counter works EVERYWHERE an iterator is expected — for loops, and every adapter:

```rust
let total: u32 = Counter { count: 0 }.sum();          // 6
let doubled: Vec<u32> = Counter { count: 0 }
    .map(|n| n * 2).collect();                        // [2, 4, 6]
for n in (Counter { count: 0 }) { /* 1,2,3 */ }
```

`type Item` is an ASSOCIATED TYPE — the kind of value this iterator produces. You set it once; the toolbox uses it.

This is the Iterator trait's superpower and a perfect example of "implement the small core, inherit the rich API". A for loop is really just sugar that calls next() until it returns None. By implementing next() you tap into a decade of polished, zero-cost iterator machinery — your custom data source instantly composes with the whole ecosystem.

