lessons.body:
So far your program has been one cook in the kitchen, doing one thing at a time. A thread is a second cook — the operating system runs both truly in parallel on different CPU cores:

```rust
use std::thread;

fn main() {
    let handle = thread::spawn(|| {
        println!("worker: chopping vegetables");
    });

    println!("main: stirring the sauce");
    handle.join().unwrap(); // wait for the worker
}
```

Two things to notice:

• spawn returns a JoinHandle — the worker's pager. Calling .join() means "wait until they're done". Skip it and main might finish first — and when main exits, ALL workers are fired mid-chop.
• The two printlns can appear in either order — that's real parallelism, the OS decides.

Now the classic question: can the worker use MY data?

```rust
let veggies = vec!["carrot", "potato"];
thread::spawn(move || {
    println!("{:?}", veggies); // worker owns it now
});
```

The `move` keyword (closures lesson!) hands ownership to the worker. Why must it? Because the worker might outlive your function — a reference could go stale mid-use. In C++ this compiles and crashes mysteriously; in Rust forgetting `move` is a compile error with a helpful hint.

That's the famous "fearless concurrency": the ownership rules you've been learning since lesson 7 turn out to be EXACTLY the rules that prevent data races. The compiler was training you for this all along.

