lessons.body:
Two cooks who grab the same pan cause accidents. The safer kitchen: workers pass NOTES through a tube instead of touching shared stuff. That's a channel:

```rust
use std::sync::mpsc;
use std::thread;

fn main() {
    let (tx, rx) = mpsc::channel();
    //   ^transmitter  ^receiver

    thread::spawn(move || {
        tx.send("order up: pancakes").unwrap();
    });

    println!("{}", rx.recv().unwrap());
}
```

tx is the tube's IN end (goes to the worker via move), rx the OUT end. recv() waits patiently until a note arrives.

The name mpsc = Multiple Producer, Single Consumer: many cooks can send (clone the transmitter — `let tx2 = tx.clone()`), one manager receives. Receiving in bulk reads beautifully:

```rust
for msg in rx {
    println!("got: {msg}");
}
// loop ends when every tx is dropped
```

Here's the ownership magic that makes channels SAFE, not just convenient: send() MOVES the value down the tube. After sending, the worker literally cannot touch that data anymore — the compiler enforces it. There is no moment where two threads both see the value. Entire categories of bugs, gone by design.

This is Rust's preferred concurrency style, borrowed from Go's slogan: "Do not communicate by sharing memory; share memory by communicating." When workers must genuinely share one thing, the next lesson's tools step in.

