lessons.body:
Channels cover most cases — but sometimes workers genuinely need ONE shared thing: a single scoreboard everyone updates. Two tools combine for this, and you've already met both of their ancestors.

Mutex<T> (mutual exclusion) is a whiteboard with exactly ONE marker. To write, you must hold the marker:

```rust
use std::sync::Mutex;

let score = Mutex::new(0);
{
    let mut s = score.lock().unwrap(); // take the marker
    *s += 10;
}   // marker returned automatically here!
```

lock() blocks until the marker is free. The brilliant part: unlocking is automatic — the guard returns the marker when it goes out of scope. The classic C bug "forgot to unlock" simply cannot be written.

But wait — to give several threads the SAME Mutex, they must all own it… multiple owners… that was Rc! Except Rc's counter isn't thread-safe. Its thread-safe twin is Arc (Atomically Reference Counted):

```rust
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};
use std::thread;

let score = Arc::new(Mutex::new(0));
let mut handles = vec![];

for _ in 0..5 {
    let score = Arc::clone(&score);
    handles.push(thread::spawn(move || {
        *score.lock().unwrap() += 1;
    }));
}
for h in handles { h.join().unwrap(); }
println!("{}", *score.lock().unwrap()); // exactly 5
```

Arc<Mutex<T>> — read it as "shared handle to a one-marker whiteboard". It's the multithreaded sibling of Rc<RefCell<T>> from the smart-pointers lesson; the pattern rhymes on purpose. Five threads, five increments, zero lost updates — guaranteed.

