lessons.body:
Threads are extra cooks. Async is something different: ONE clever cook who never stands still. Start the rice cooker → instead of staring at it, chop vegetables → rice beeps → come back. One worker, many overlapping waits.

That's the async sweet spot: tasks that mostly WAIT (network requests, file reads, timers). A web server juggling 10,000 idle connections doesn't need 10,000 cooks — it needs one or two who switch between pots efficiently.

```rust
use tokio::time::{sleep, Duration};

async fn brew(name: &str, ms: u64) {
    sleep(Duration::from_millis(ms)).await;
    println!("{name} ready!");
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    brew("tea", 200).await;        // one at a time

    tokio::join!(                  // overlapping!
        brew("coffee", 200),
        brew("toast", 200),
    );
}
```

The vocabulary, piece by piece:

• async fn — returns a Future: a recipe card describing work, not the work itself. Like iterators, futures are LAZY — nothing happens until…
• .await — "run this, and while it waits, go do something useful elsewhere." The cook walks away from the rice cooker.
• tokio — the kitchen manager (an async runtime). Plain Rust ships the async syntax but not the scheduler; tokio is the one nearly everyone uses. #[tokio::main] sets up the kitchen.
• join! — start several futures and wait for all: the coffee and toast overlap, finishing together in ~200ms, not 400.

When to choose which: mostly-waiting work (I/O) → async. Number-crunching that keeps a CPU core busy → threads. Big real systems use both.

