lessons.body:
Time to explain the ! you've typed ten thousand times. println!, vec!, format! — every one is a macro: code that writes code at compile time.

Why can't println! be a normal function? Count its arguments: println!("{}", x) takes two, println!("{} {}", x, y) takes three. Rust functions have FIXED parameter counts — but a macro receives the raw code you wrote and EXPANDS into real code before compilation. Variable arguments, checked format strings: things functions cannot do.

You can write your own with macro_rules! — it's pattern matching on code itself:

```rust
macro_rules! shout {
    ($msg:expr) => {
        println!("{}!!!", $msg.to_uppercase())
    };
}

shout!("hello");      // HELLO!!!
shout!("ship it");    // SHIP IT!!!
```

Read the pattern: $msg:expr means "capture any expression, call it $msg". The right side is the template that replaces the call. At compile time, shout!("hello") literally BECOMES the println! line — zero runtime cost.

The macros you'll USE most often, though, are derive macros:

```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]
struct Crop {
    name: String,
    days: u32,
}
```

One line, and the compiler WRITES the debug-printing, cloning, and comparison implementations for you. (That's why {:?} printing "just worked" all course — derive was writing impls behind your back. serde's derives even generate complete JSON serializers.)

When should you WRITE macros? Rarely — prefer plain functions until you genuinely need variable arguments, code generation, or a mini-language. But READING them confidently? That you now can.

