lessons.body:
Ownership says each value has ONE owner. But to share data among several threads, you need MANY owners — each thread co-owning the data, with it staying alive until the last thread is done. That is exactly what Arc provides.

Arc means "Atomically Reference Counted". It keeps a COUNT of how many owners exist. Clone the Arc and the count goes up (a cheap pointer copy, NOT a copy of the data); drop one and the count goes down. When it hits zero, the data is freed. The "Atomic" part means this counting is thread-safe.

```rust
use std::sync::Arc;
use std::thread;

let data = Arc::new(vec![1, 2, 3]);

for i in 0..3 {
    let data = Arc::clone(&data);   // bump the count, share the SAME vec
    thread::spawn(move || {
        println!("thread {i} sees {:?}", data);
    });
}
```

Each thread gets its own Arc handle pointing at the one shared Vec. The `move` hands that handle into the thread; the data lives until every thread finishes.

You may recall Rc from the smart-pointers lessons — Arc is its thread-safe twin. The difference:
• Rc — reference counting for a SINGLE thread (faster, no atomic overhead).
• Arc — reference counting ACROSS threads (atomic, slightly slower, but safe to share).

The compiler enforces this: try to send an Rc to another thread and it refuses, because Rc is not thread-safe. Use Arc for threads, Rc otherwise.

The famous combination is Arc<Mutex<T>> — Arc to SHARE the data across threads, Mutex to safely MUTATE it:

```rust
let shared = Arc::new(Mutex::new(0));
// each thread: let s = Arc::clone(&shared); ... *s.lock().unwrap() += 1;
```

Read it inside-out: a Mutex protecting a value, wrapped in an Arc so many threads can own it. That pairing — Arc for sharing, Mutex for mutation — is THE standard Rust pattern for shared mutable state across threads, with data races impossible by construction.

