lessons.body:
Sometimes you have a big pile of independent work — process a million items, each computation separate. Threads and channels would work but are fiddly to set up. Rayon makes it almost EFFORTLESS: turn a sequential iterator into a parallel one by changing one word.

```rust
use rayon::prelude::*;

// sequential
let sum: i32 = numbers.iter().map(|n| expensive(n)).sum();

// parallel — across all CPU cores
let sum: i32 = numbers.par_iter().map(|n| expensive(n)).sum();
```

`iter()` became `par_iter()`. That is the whole change. Rayon splits the work across all your CPU cores, runs the closures in parallel, and combines the results — and because the API mirrors normal iterators, your map/filter/sum/collect chains work unchanged.

How is this SAFE? The same Send/Sync rules. Rayon requires the closures and data to be thread-safe, so the compiler rejects any version that could race. You get parallelism with the same guarantees as single-threaded code — no manual locks, no data races.

Under the hood Rayon uses WORK-STEALING: idle cores grab pending work from busy ones, keeping every core fed without you managing threads at all. It scales automatically to however many cores the machine has.

The sweet spot is DATA PARALLELISM — many independent items, each needing real CPU work:

```rust
let results: Vec<_> = images.par_iter()
    .map(|img| resize(img))     // each image resized on some core
    .collect();
```

Caveats worth knowing: parallelism has overhead, so it only pays off when the per-item work is substantial OR the collection is large — par_iter over three tiny additions is slower than sequential. And the work must be genuinely INDEPENDENT (no shared mutable state between items, or you are back to needing Mutex).

The mental model: reach for Rayon when you catch yourself thinking "this loop is slow and each iteration is independent". One word — par_iter — and you are using every core, safely.

