lessons.body:
Time to build an actual neuron — the LEGO brick of every neural network, including the giant ones behind ChatGPT.

Picture a nightclub bouncer deciding who gets in. He checks a few things and weighs them differently: dress code matters a lot (weight 3), being on the guest list matters even more (weight 5), arriving early barely matters (weight 1). He adds up the weighted score in his head — over the threshold, you're in.

That bouncer IS a neuron:

```rust
let sum = w1 * x1 + w2 * x2 + bias;
let output = if sum > 0.0 { 1.0 }
             else { 0.0 };
```

• x1, x2 — the facts about the input
• w1, w2 — how much each fact matters (the weights)
• bias — how strict the threshold is overall
• the if — the "activation": fire or don't

Here's the beautiful part — the bouncer LEARNS from mistakes. Wrongly turned someone away? Loosen the weights that caused it. Let in a troublemaker? Tighten them:

```rust
let error = target - output;
w1 += lr * error * x1;
w2 += lr * error * x2;
bias += lr * error;
```

Notice: if the prediction was right, error is 0 and nothing changes. Wrong, and every weight gets nudged toward the correct answer. Loop over your examples ~50 times, and the weights settle into values that just... work. The weights ARE the knowledge.

This single neuron is the perceptron — invented in 1958! Stack thousands into layers, smooth out that harsh if, train with gradient descent, and you get deep learning. Scale to billions and you get LLMs. Same brick, bigger castle. (In Rust, the big castles are built with Burn and Candle — next lessons.)

