lessons.body:
GPIO = General Purpose Input/Output — the chip's fingers. Each pin can be configured as:

• an OUTPUT: your code sets it high (3.3V) or low (0V) — turning LEDs, relays and motors on and off.
• an INPUT: your code reads whether the outside world is high or low — buttons, switches, sensors.

```rust
// real RP2040 code looks like this:
let mut led = pins.gpio25.into_push_pull_output();
let button = pins.gpio15.into_pull_up_input();

if button.is_low() {   // pressed!
    led.set_high();
}
```

Two real-world gotchas every embedded dev learns:

1. Floating pins. An unconnected input picks up radio noise and reads random values — like asking an undecided voter every millisecond. The fix is a pull-up resistor: it gently holds the pin HIGH until a button press pulls it LOW. (That's why pressed often reads `is_low` — counterintuitive but standard.)

2. State machines. Real devices are "in a state" — a traffic light is Red, Green, or Yellow, never in between. Rust enums + match model this perfectly, and the compiler proves you handled every state. This is THE embedded pattern, and it's your project.

