lessons.body:
Here's the wall every embedded beginner hits. Your blinky uses delay_ms(500) — which BLOCKS. The chip stands frozen, counting milliseconds. Now blink LED A every 500ms and LED B every 300ms… with one core and blocking delays, you're writing a scheduling headache by hand.

The async course taught you the answer: one clever cook. Embassy brings exactly that to microcontrollers — async/await with no OS, no threads, in no_std:

```rust
#[embassy_executor::task]
async fn blink_a(mut led: Output<'static>) {
    loop {
        led.toggle();
        Timer::after_millis(500).await;
    }
}

#[embassy_executor::task]
async fn blink_b(mut led: Output<'static>) {
    loop {
        led.toggle();
        Timer::after_millis(300).await;
    }
}
```

Two independent tasks, each beautifully simple — spawn both and they interleave perfectly. The .await is the magic moment: instead of freezing the chip, the task steps aside, the executor runs whoever is due, and a hardware timer wakes things up on schedule.

Even better for battery projects: when NO task is due, Embassy puts the chip into a low-power sleep until the next deadline. Your async code is greener than the hand-written loop.

A button-watching task completes the picture:

```rust
async fn watch(mut btn: Input<'static>) {
    loop {
        btn.wait_for_falling_edge().await;
        println!("pressed!");
    }
}
```

No polling loop burning cycles — the task literally sleeps until the hardware says "now". Embassy is why modern embedded Rust reads like application code while behaving like firmware.

