lessons.body:
A microcontroller with no screen still needs to talk — to your laptop, to a GPS module, to another chip. The oldest and simplest way is UART: a serial connection sending one bit at a time over a single wire each direction.

UART uses just two data lines:
• TX — transmit (this device's output)
• RX — receive (this device's input)

Crucially, they CROSS: one device's TX connects to the other's RX. Forgetting to cross them is the number-one "why is nothing working?" mistake.

Both sides must agree on the BAUD RATE — the speed in bits per second. 9600 and 115200 are classics. If the two ends disagree, you get garbage characters. There is no shared clock line, so the baud rate IS the agreement that keeps them in sync.

In code, a UART is treated like a stream of bytes:

```rust
// send
uart.write_all(b"Hello\r\n").unwrap();

// receive one byte
let mut buf = [0u8; 1];
uart.read(&mut buf).unwrap();
```

Note `b"Hello"` — a BYTE STRING, since UART moves raw bytes, not Rust String types. Embedded code lives in bytes.

This is also how you DEBUG without a screen: wire up a USB-to-serial adapter, write log messages over UART, and watch them in a serial terminal on your computer. UART-as-printf is the embedded developer's oldest debugging friend — when you cannot use println!, you send bytes down the wire instead.

