lessons.body:
A sensor on a coin cell might need to run for YEARS. The single biggest factor is power management: a microcontroller that sleeps cleverly can last a thousand times longer than one that spins in a busy loop.

The cardinal sin of embedded power is the BUSY-WAIT:

```rust
loop {
    if button_pressed() { do_something(); }
    // CPU runs full speed doing nothing — battery drains fast
}
```

The CPU burns full current just to keep asking "anything yet?". Instead, SLEEP and let hardware wake you:

```rust
loop {
    do_work();
    cortex_m::asm::wfi(); // Wait For Interrupt — CPU halts, low power
}
```

`wfi` ("wait for interrupt") stops the core until an interrupt fires — a button press, a timer, incoming data. While asleep the chip might draw microamps instead of milliamps: a 1000× difference.

Most chips offer SLEEP DEPTHS, trading wake-up speed for savings:
• Sleep / Idle — CPU off, peripherals on. Wakes instantly. Modest savings.
• Stop / Deep sleep — most clocks off, RAM retained. Big savings, slower wake.
• Standby — nearly everything off, minimal state kept. Lowest power, slowest wake (often a near-reset).

The design pattern for battery devices is "sleep by default, wake to work": stay in the deepest sleep you can, wake on an interrupt (a timer or a sensor), do the minimum, and go back to sleep. The less time awake, the longer the battery lives.

This is where Rust's zero-cost abstractions pay off: high-level, readable code compiles down to the same tight instructions you'd hand-write, so you get safety AND microamp battery life.

