lessons.body:
A test is a robot that re-checks your code forever. Write it once, and that bug can never sneak back — that's why teams treat tests as the price of admission for merging code.

Rust builds testing in, no extra tools:

```rust
fn tip(bill: f64, percent: f64) -> f64 {
    bill * percent / 100.0
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn fifteen_percent_of_100() {
        assert_eq!(tip(100.0, 15.0), 15.0);
    }

    #[test]
    fn zero_bill_zero_tip() {
        assert_eq!(tip(0.0, 15.0), 0.0);
    }
}
```

The pieces: #[test] marks a robot; assert_eq!(actual, expected) is its check — mismatch = test fails, with both values printed. The #[cfg(test)] wrapper means "compile this module only for cargo test" — robots never ship in your binary.

`cargo test` runs every robot, in PARALLEL (your fearless concurrency at work), and reports green or red. More tools in the belt:

• assert!(condition) — for boolean checks
• #[should_panic] — "this test passes IF the code panics" (testing your fire alarms!)
• cargo test tip — run only tests whose names contain "tip"
• tests/ folder — integration tests that use your library like a stranger would, public API only

The team workflow that makes it click: found a bug? FIRST write the failing test that exposes it, THEN fix until green. The bug is now extinct — the robot guards its grave.

