lessons.body:
Two final production skills: organizing BIG projects, and giving code back to the ecosystem you've been drawing from all course.

Workspaces — when one crate gets crowded, split it. A workspace is several crates developed together, sharing one Cargo.lock and one target/ build folder:

```rust
[workspace]
members = ["server", "cli", "shared-types"]
```

The server and CLI both depend on shared-types by path; cargo build at the root builds everything, consistently versioned. Every sizeable Rust project you'll meet — including the compiler itself — is a workspace.

Publishing — remember adding `rand = "0.8"` and it just worked? Someone published that. Your turn requires:

Documentation with /// comments — they're markdown, and they ship:

```rust
/// Returns the largest value in the slice.
///
/// # Examples
/// ```
/// assert_eq!(largest(&[1, 5, 3]), &5);
/// ```
pub fn largest(...) {}
```

`cargo doc --open` renders the same beautiful docs you've browsed on docs.rs. And the magic trick: that # Examples code block IS A TEST — cargo test compiles and runs it. Documentation that can never lie.

Then: fill [package] metadata (description, license, repository), `cargo publish`, done — the world can now `cargo add yourcrate`.

One forever-rule: published versions are PERMANENT. No deleting, no overwriting (the ecosystem must be able to rebuild old code). Made a mistake? `cargo yank` hides a version from NEW projects without breaking existing ones. Semver discipline starts mattering the moment strangers depend on you.

