lessons.body:
Rust is fast — but "I think this is faster" is not engineering. The professional habit is to MEASURE before optimizing, because intuition about performance is wrong more often than it is right.

First, a non-negotiable: benchmark in RELEASE mode. Debug builds skip optimizations and can be 10–100× slower, so debug timings are meaningless.

```
cargo run --release
cargo build --release
```

For real measurement, do not hand-roll timers — use `criterion`, the standard benchmarking crate. It runs your code many times, discards warm-up runs, accounts for noise, and reports statistically sound results with confidence intervals:

```rust
// benches/my_bench.rs
fn bench_parse(c: &mut Criterion) {
    c.bench_function("parse", |b| {
        b.iter(|| parse(black_box(INPUT)))
    });
}
```

`black_box` stops the compiler from "optimizing away" code whose result you ignore — a classic way to accidentally benchmark nothing.

BENCHMARKING tells you how fast; PROFILING tells you WHERE the time goes. Tools like `perf`, `flamegraph`, and `samply` show which functions dominate, so you spend effort on the real bottleneck. This is the 90/10 rule: usually ~10% of the code accounts for ~90% of the time. Optimizing the other 90% is wasted effort.

The disciplined loop: measure (profile to find the hot spot) → optimize that one spot → measure again to confirm it actually helped. Skipping the final measurement is how "optimizations" silently make things slower. Premature optimization — tuning code before measuring — wastes time and obscures the real problem.

