lessons.body:
Whether you publish a crate or just share a module with teammates, your public API is a PROMISE. Designing it thoughtfully — and documenting it well — is what separates a library people enjoy from one they fight.

THE GOLDEN RULE: make the right thing easy and the wrong thing hard. A well-designed API guides callers into correct usage through its types.

• Accept flexible inputs, return concrete types. Take `&str` or `impl AsRef<str>` rather than `String`; take `impl IntoIterator` rather than `Vec`. This is "be liberal in what you accept".
• Use the TYPE SYSTEM to prevent mistakes. A function taking `Celsius` and `Fahrenheit` newtypes cannot mix them up; one taking two `f64`s can. Make illegal states unrepresentable.
• Return `Result` for fallible operations, not panics. Let the caller decide how to handle failure.

VISIBILITY is part of design. Everything is private by default; you opt into `pub`. Expose the minimum — a small public surface is easier to keep stable and free to refactor behind. Once something is `pub` in a released crate, changing it is a breaking change.

DOCUMENTATION is not optional for a public API. Every `pub` item gets a `///` doc comment, and the best docs include a runnable example:

```rust
/// Returns the area of a circle.
///
/// # Examples
/// ```
/// assert_eq!(area(2.0), 12.566370614359172);
/// ```
pub fn area(r: f64) -> f64 { std::f64::consts::PI * r * r }
```

Those examples in ``` fences are DOCTESTS — `cargo test` actually runs them, so your documentation can never silently go stale. Add `#![deny(missing_docs)]` to force every public item to be documented. The mark of a professional crate: a small, well-typed public surface, Result-based errors, and docs with examples that the test suite keeps honest.

