lessons.body:
Capstone time — real programs that combine everything. First up: the guessing game, the traditional first complete Rust program. The computer picks a secret number; you guess; it says higher or lower.

Sounds tiny, but look at the toolkit it exercises: loop, match, Result, parsing, the rand crate, and a new friend — Ordering:

```rust
use std::cmp::Ordering;

match guess.cmp(&secret) {
    Ordering::Less => println!("higher!"),
    Ordering::Greater => println!("lower!"),
    Ordering::Equal => {
        println!("you win!");
        break;
    }
}
```

cmp compares any two ordered values and returns Ordering — an enum with exactly three variants. A match on it is exhaustive, so "forgot the equal case" can't happen. Enums + match doing what they were born for.

The secret comes from the rand crate:

```rust
use rand::Rng;
let secret = rand::thread_rng().gen_range(1..=100);
```

In the terminal version, guesses come from the keyboard — and keyboard text is ALWAYS a parsing hazard:

```rust
let guess: u32 = match input.trim().parse() {
    Ok(n) => n,
    Err(_) => continue, // not a number? next try!
};
```

That match-on-parse is the move to remember: bad input doesn't crash the game, it just asks again. (The Playground has no keyboard input, so your build feeds guesses from a list — a binary-search strategy, in fact. Same logic, simulated player.)

