lessons.body:
Every developer builds a todo app; here's the Rust-shaped one. The design — and notice how every piece is a lesson you've done:

```rust
struct Todo {
    title: String,
    done: bool,
}

struct App {
    todos: Vec<Todo>,
}

impl App {
    fn add(&mut self, title: &str) { /* push */ }
    fn complete(&mut self, i: usize) -> bool { /* get_mut */ }
    fn pending(&self) -> usize { /* iterator! */ }
}
```

Structs model the data, impl gives behavior, Vec stores the list, &mut self for mutations, iterators for stats:

```rust
fn pending(&self) -> usize {
    self.todos.iter()
        .filter(|t| !t.done)
        .count()
}
```

In the real terminal version, two more pieces complete it:

• CLI arguments — `todo add "water crops"` arrives via std::env::args(); match on the subcommand to route.
• Persistence — the list must survive restarts. The ecosystem answer is serde: tag the structs with #[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)] (derive macros!) and serde_json turns the whole Vec into a JSON file and back in two lines.

One design taste-test you'll face: `done: bool` works today, but what about a "started" state tomorrow? Many Rustaceans reach straight for `enum Status { Pending, Started, Done }` — enums make new states a compile-checked refactor instead of a bug hunt. Your TODO list includes exactly that upgrade.

