lessons.body:
grep is the terminal tool that finds lines containing a word. Building "minigrep" is THE Rust Book rite of passage, because a real search tool exercises the subtle stuff: lifetimes, Result-driven design, and environment config.

The heart is one beautiful function:

```rust
fn search<'a>(query: &str, contents: &'a str)
    -> Vec<&'a str>
{
    contents.lines()
        .filter(|line| line.contains(query))
        .collect()
}
```

Look closely at that lifetime — it's the lifetimes lesson paying rent. The returned lines are VIEWS into `contents` (not copies!), so the output is tied to 'a, the contents' due-date. Not tied to query — the query can die immediately after; the results don't reference it. The annotation documents exactly which input the output borrows from.

Around the core, production-shaped structure:

```rust
struct Config { query: String, path: String }

impl Config {
    fn build(args: &[String]) -> Result<Config, String> {
        // missing args -> Err("usage: minigrep query file")
    }
}
```

Config::build returns Result — bad arguments produce a friendly usage message, not a panic (the production error-handling lesson, applied). File reading is one line: std::fs::read_to_string(path)? — with ? bubbling the "file not found" up to main.

Final touch: case-insensitive mode toggled by an environment variable (std::env::var("IGNORE_CASE")) — lowercase both sides and compare. You'll build exactly that variant in the project.

