lessons.body:
Model a small bank: accounts that deposit, withdraw, and transfer, with every operation validated. This capstone is all about using the TYPE SYSTEM and Result to make a correct, hard-to-misuse domain model — exactly how real financial code is structured.

Start with the data. A struct for the account, and an ENUM for the kinds of transactions — so each transaction is exactly one valid shape:

```rust
struct Account { owner: String, balance: u64 }  // cents, never floats!

enum Transaction {
    Deposit(u64),
    Withdraw(u64),
    Transfer { to: String, amount: u64 },
}
```

Two professional habits hiding in there:
• MONEY AS INTEGERS (cents), never floats — floating point cannot represent 0.10 exactly, and rounding errors in money are unacceptable. Store cents as u64 and format the dollars on display.
• Each operation returns a Result, so an OVERDRAFT (withdrawing more than the balance) is an Err, not a silent negative balance:

```rust
impl Account {
    fn withdraw(&mut self, amount: u64) -> Result<(), String> {
        if amount > self.balance {
            return Err(format!("insufficient funds: have {}, need {}", self.balance, amount));
        }
        self.balance -= amount;
        Ok(())
    }
}
```

Methods take &mut self because they CHANGE the balance. The Result return makes every caller acknowledge that an operation can fail.

Design ideas to explore:
• A ledger: keep a Vec<Transaction> as an audit trail of everything that happened.
• Apply a list of transactions in sequence, stopping (or logging) on the first error.
• A transfer is really a withdraw from one account plus a deposit to another — compose the smaller operations.

This capstone cements the core of robust Rust: model the domain with structs and enums so bad states cannot exist, return Result so failures are explicit, and keep methods small and composable. The same shape underlies inventory systems, game economies, and yes, actual banking software. Get the types right and the logic follows.

