lessons.body:
Build a sensor-data analyzer: feed it a series of temperature readings and have it report the min, max, average, and which readings were unusually warm. This capstone is iterators and statistics in action — the bread and butter of data tools.

Model a reading as a struct (a label plus a value), and keep a Vec of them:

```rust
struct Reading { hour: u8, temp: f64 }

let readings = vec![
    Reading { hour: 0, temp: 12.5 },
    Reading { hour: 6, temp: 18.0 },
    // ...
];
```

Now the statistics fall out of iterator methods — no manual loops needed:

```rust
let temps: Vec<f64> = readings.iter().map(|r| r.temp).collect();

let count = temps.len();
let sum: f64 = temps.iter().sum();
let avg = sum / count as f64;
let max = temps.iter().cloned().fold(f64::MIN, f64::max);
let min = temps.iter().cloned().fold(f64::MIN.abs(), f64::min);
```

A subtlety worth knowing: floats do not implement Ord (because of NaN), so the usual `.max()` on an iterator does not work directly for f64. The fold-with-f64::max trick sidesteps it; alternatively use `.max_by(|a, b| a.partial_cmp(b).unwrap())`.

Then filter for the interesting readings — those above the average, or above a threshold:

```rust
let hot: Vec<&Reading> = readings.iter()
    .filter(|r| r.temp > avg)
    .collect();
```

Extensions that make it feel real:
• A moving average to smooth noisy data.
• Detect the biggest hour-to-hour JUMP using windows(2) over the slice.
• Group readings by part of day with a HashMap.

The takeaway: most data analysis is "map the field you care about, then reduce it to a statistic, then filter for what matters". With Rust's iterators that reads almost like the sentence describing it — and runs at full speed. This is the same shape as analyzing server metrics, stock prices, or fitness-tracker data. Compute one statistic at a time, verify it against a tiny dataset you can check by hand, then build up.

