lessons.body:
Build the brain of a URL shortener like bit.ly: take a long URL, hand back a short code, and look the original back up from the code. It is a compact, satisfying project that shows two HashMaps, encoding, and a clean API working together.

The core is a bidirectional mapping:
• code → long URL (to redirect visitors).
• long URL → code (so the same URL reuses its existing code instead of making a new one).

```rust
struct Shortener {
    to_url: HashMap<String, String>,  // code  -> url
    to_code: HashMap<String, String>, // url   -> code
    next_id: u64,
}
```

GENERATING A CODE. The classic trick is base-62 encoding of an incrementing counter — digits plus lowercase plus uppercase (62 characters) make short, URL-safe codes. Counter 0 → "a", 125 → "cb", and so on. Even six base-62 characters encode over 56 billion URLs.

```rust
fn encode(mut n: u64) -> String {
    const CHARS: &[u8] = b"abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789";
    if n == 0 { return "a".to_string(); }
    let mut s = Vec::new();
    while n > 0 {
        s.push(CHARS[(n % 62) as usize]);
        n /= 62;
    }
    String::from_utf8(s).unwrap()
}
```

THE API. Two clean methods:
• shorten(url) → returns the existing code if the URL is already known, otherwise mints a new one and stores both directions.
• resolve(code) → returns Option<&String>, since the code might not exist (use .get(), never indexing, so a bad code yields None instead of panicking).

```rust
fn resolve(&self, code: &str) -> Option<&String> {
    self.to_url.get(code)
}
```

Extensions that mirror the real thing: track a HIT COUNT per code (another HashMap), reject invalid URLs up front, or persist the maps to a file so links survive a restart.

This capstone pulls together HashMaps, ownership of Strings, Option-based lookups, and a small, intention-revealing API — the same ingredients behind countless web services. Build shorten and resolve first with a fixed counter, confirm a round-trip works, then add the base-62 encoder and reuse logic.

