lessons.body:
Bread routes events by dotted name: `bread.device.connected`, `bread.power.ac.connected`. Modules subscribe to a pattern, not one exact string.

The matcher lives once in `bread-shared::glob` so dispatch and `bread events --filter` cannot disagree:

- `*` matches exactly one segment, never a dot.
- `**` matches zero or more segments.
- `?` matches one character, never a dot.

```rust
assert!(matches_pattern("bread.device.*", "bread.device.connected"));
assert!(!matches_pattern("bread.*", "bread.device.connected"));
assert!(matches_pattern("bread.**", "bread.device.connected"));
assert!(matches_pattern("bread.**", "bread"));
```

The table keeps subscriptions in a vector and an id-to-index map:

```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, Eq, PartialEq, Hash)]
pub struct SubscriptionId(pub u64);

pub struct SubscriptionTable {
    entries: Vec<Subscription>,
    by_id: HashMap<SubscriptionId, usize>,
}
```

Removal is where the subtle bug lives. `swap_remove` fills the hole with the last element, so the index map must be repaired:

```rust
self.entries.swap_remove(idx);
if idx < self.entries.len() {
    let swapped = self.entries[idx].id;
    self.by_id.remove(&swapped);
    self.by_id.insert(swapped, idx);
}
```

Forget the repair and an unrelated subscription becomes unreachable. Dispatch is then a filter over the table and a clone of the matches, so handlers can be called after the borrow ends. A newtype such as `SubscriptionId(pub u64)` prevents mixing ids with other counters at the type level.

