lessons.body:
`unsafe` does not turn off the borrow checker. It grants five abilities: dereference raw pointers, call `unsafe` functions, touch mutable statics, implement `unsafe` traits, and access union fields. Everything else stays checked.

That means every `unsafe` block is a proof obligation. The block is sound only if all callers of the enclosing API cannot break the invariants the compiler normally enforces: a `&mut T` is exclusive, a reference is aligned and points to a live initialized value of the right type, and no data race is possible.

The standard library's `split_at_mut` is the classic example:

```rust
fn split_at_mut<T>(values: &mut [T], mid: usize) -> (&mut [T], &mut [T]) {
    let len = values.len();
    let ptr = values.as_mut_ptr();
    assert!(mid <= len);
    // SAFETY: mid <= len, both ranges are inside the same allocation,
    // are disjoint, aligned and initialized, and the borrow of `values`
    // guarantees no other reference aliases them.
    unsafe {
        (
            std::slice::from_raw_parts_mut(ptr, mid),
            std::slice::from_raw_parts_mut(ptr.add(mid), len - mid),
        )
    }
}
```

The `assert!` is part of the proof. Without it, `mid > len` would build an invalid slice and any later use is undefined behaviour.

Practical rules:

- Prefer safe wrappers (`bytemuck`, `zerocopy`, `MaybeUninit` helpers) to hand-written pointer code.
- `MaybeUninit` for uninitialized memory; call `assume_init` only after every byte is initialized.
- `transmute` is almost always the wrong tool: sizes and validity invariants must match exactly.
- Run `cargo +nightly miri test` on unsafe code. Miri catches aliasing violations that tests and the borrow checker cannot.
- Document `# Safety` on every `unsafe fn`: what the caller must guarantee, not what the function does.

An `unsafe` block with no comment is a bug report waiting to happen.

